  "query_preview": "..(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 430 AM EDT WED SEP 16 2026\n\nThe extended portion of the forecast looks to be on track with \nthe idea of the large 5h ridge weakening and drifting southwest\nand out of the region enough through the weekend for more \nintrusions of energy at mid levels. This should keep a threat of \nmainly unorganized convection in the picture even as we remain \nunseasonable warm.\n\nThe previous long term discussion follows:\n\nHigh pressure and associated ridge axis aloft over eastern Kentucky \nwill retrograde and weaken with time, allowing for increasingly \nfaster west-northwesterly flow aloft through the Ohio Valley as the \nlong-term period progresses through the weekend. \n\nWarm and mostly dry conditions are expected Thursday as strong mid-\nlevel heights exceeding 597 dm gradually decrease, though low \nchances for showers and thunderstorms are warranted across parts \nof mainly central and northeastern Kentucky and perhaps extending \nsouth to near the Cumberland Mountains in the afternoon. Highs are\nexpected to reach the upper 80s to lower 90s across the area, with\nmax heat indices in the vicinity of 100 degrees.\n\nA passing disturbance to the north and associated jet streak will \npush a cold front far enough south Friday to bring increased shower \nand thunderstorm chances across the area along with a brief \nmodest cooldown in the northern half of the forecast area, but \nhighs will still reach the mid 80s to lower 90s. \n\nWarm advection quickly returns for Saturday, with low PoPs and a \nreturn to highs back into the upper 80s to lower 90s, which looks to \nextend through Sunday before a potential cooldown as an upper \ntrough and associated cold front move into the area and increase \nrain chances. While models show broad 40-50 PoPs Monday into \nMonday night with relatively good agreement in a system moving \nfrom the central CONUS to the Great Lakes region, model ensemble \nsystems show significant disagreements on the strength, track, and\ntiming of the parent trough. Resolving these differences will\nlikely play a key role in whether those PoPs trend upwards or \ndownwards as this period nears.",
